WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.291
Hi, I'm Sterling!

00:00:02.291 --> 00:00:05.627
Nearly every language model descends from this one block, stacked.

00:00:05.627 --> 00:00:06.962
Here's how it works.

00:00:09.517 --> 00:00:11.332
Each token carries one vector.

00:00:11.332 --> 00:00:15.689
Every block adds to it twice: attention reads earlier tokens, then an

00:00:15.689 --> 00:00:17.867
MLP works on each token alone.

00:00:19.925 --> 00:00:23.136
Each token id picks a row of an embedding table.

00:00:23.136 --> 00:00:25.063
That vector starts its residual stream.

00:00:25.483 --> 00:00:30.157
Attention reads a normalised copy, gathers from earlier tokens, and adds back.

00:00:30.577 --> 00:00:35.254
The MLP does the same per token: normalise, widen four times, narrow, add.

00:00:35.674 --> 00:00:39.771
After the last block, one matrix scores every token in the vocabulary.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:42.724
One line does the reading.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.277
Every query meets every key: one score per pair.

00:00:46.697 --> 00:00:49.264
Divide by the root of the head width.

00:00:49.264 --> 00:00:51.831
Otherwise the scores spread, and softmax goes one-hot.

00:00:52.251 --> 00:00:56.180
Softmax makes each row sum to one, and those weights average the values.

00:00:57.667 --> 00:01:00.274
Unmasked, every token could read its own answer.

00:01:00.694 --> 00:01:03.010
So future scores become minus infinity.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:07.642
Each token sees only its past, and one pass trains every position.

00:01:09.083 --> 00:01:10.069
Stack the block.

00:01:10.069 --> 00:01:13.683
Llama 2 seven B has thirty-two, all writing to one stream.

00:01:14.103 --> 00:01:16.770
Per block, the MLP holds two thirds of the weights.

00:01:17.933 --> 00:01:22.482
Generating, earlier keys and values never change, so each layer caches them.

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:27.016
That's over a quarter of a million numbers per token.

00:01:27.016 --> 00:01:29.522
A full context needs over two billion bytes.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:36.882
Attention moves information between tokens, the MLP transforms each one, and

00:01:36.882 --> 00:01:38.529
the stream carries both.

00:01:40.775 --> 00:01:42.357
Attention, masked to the past.

00:01:42.357 --> 00:01:44.256
A stream every sublayer adds to.

00:01:44.256 --> 00:01:45.839
A cache for the past.

00:01:46.259 --> 00:01:48.465
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:48.465 --> 00:01:49.095
I'm Sterling.

00:01:49.095 --> 00:01:49.410
Bye!
